  <content>
  <section><title>Warm Up Activity</title>
<para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/6a0cf9a79225292448186aefaf6412a2356cbe95"/></media></figure></para>

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Explain why the diagram shows that \(6(3+4)=6 \cdot 3+6 \cdot 4\).</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>The entire diagram represents the product \(6(3+4)\). The upper half represents \(6 \cdot 3\) while the lower half represents \(6 \cdot 4\).</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Draw a diagram to show that \(5(x + 2) = 5x + 10\).</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your Answer:</para>
  <para>Diagrams will vary, but should show a large rectangle with two sub-rectangles, with one sub-rectangle representing \(5 \cdot x\), and the other representing \(5 \cdot 2\). For example:</para>
  <para><figure><media alt="A RECTANGLE DIVIDED INTO TWO SMALLER RECTANGLES. THE LENGTH OF THE RECTANGLE IS 5, THE WIDTH OF ONE SMALLER RECTANGLE IS X, AND THE WIDTH OF THE OTHER SMALLER RECTANGLE IS 2."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/fd20ae615b762081a23167872994173f39721d7d"/></media></figure></para>
</solution></exercise>
</example></section>
